---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { path } from '../lib/paths';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---

<Page
  title="Code of conduct"
  description="What is expected of contributors to MathemAct, what is not tolerated, and what happens when the line is crossed."
  lead="Scepticism is the point of this site. Contempt is not. This page is the difference between them."
  updated="August 2026"
>
  <p class="status">
    This is the short version, and it is the one that matters day to day. The full text —
    adapted from the Contributor Covenant, with the enforcement ladder written out — is
    <a href={`${SITE.repository}/blob/main/docs/code-of-conduct.md`}>in the repository</a>.
    Where the two differ, the full text governs and this page is what needs fixing.
  </p>

  <h2>Scope</h2>

  <p>
    This applies to everything posted here — reports, comments, ratings, flags — and to
    messages sent to the addresses on this site. It applies to everyone equally, including
    whoever is doing the moderating.
  </p>

  <h2>What is expected</h2>

  <dl class="items">
    <dt>Argue with the account, not the person</dt>
    <dd>
      <q>This verification step does not rule out the error I would worry about</q> is the
      kind of comment this site is for.
      <q>Anyone who trusts a chatbot with a proof is not a serious mathematician</q> is not.
      The first can be answered; the second only makes people stop posting.
    </dd>

    <dt>Assume the account is honest until you have reason not to</dt>
    <dd>
      People are describing their own work, often work that did not go well. Doubt a
      claim, ask what was checked, ask for the transcript — but start from the assumption
      that the person is flagging in good faith.
    </dd>

    <dt>Respect pseudonyms absolutely</dt>
    <dd>
      Admitting to heavy AI use still carries professional risk, and some of the most
      valuable accounts here will only ever be posted under a pseudonym. Do not try to
      work out who someone is, do not speculate publicly about it, and do not hint that
      you have guessed. This is the rule most likely to get an account removed without a
      warning first.
    </dd>

    <dt>Do not weaponise a disclosure</dt>
    <dd>
      Someone who posts here has told you something about their report that they were
      under no obligation to tell anyone. Using it against them — in a referee report, a
      hiring discussion, a grant panel, or online — defeats the entire purpose of the
      project.
    </dd>

    <dt>Post other people's material only if you may</dt>
    <dd>
      Transcript excerpts must have third-party unpublished material removed before you
      paste them. Unpublished proofs, referee reports, and private correspondence are not
      yours to reproduce, whoever typed them into the model.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>What is not acceptable</h2>

  <ul class="tombstone-list">
    <li>Harassment, intimidation, or sustained hostility toward a person.</li>
    <li>
      Attempting to identify a pseudonymous contributor, or publishing anyone's personal
      information.
    </li>
    <li>
      Demeaning remarks about someone's nationality, gender, religion, disability, age,
      institution, or career stage.
    </li>
    <li>
      Posting an account of work you did not do, or fabricating a transcript, an outcome,
      or a verification step.
    </li>
    <li>Promotion of a product, a service, or yourself in place of a genuine account.</li>
    <li>Reproducing unpublished material belonging to someone who has not agreed.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Disagreement is not on that list, and will not be added to it. Reporting that a widely
    praised tool failed you, or that a widely condemned one worked, is a contribution and
    is treated as one.
  </p>

  <h2>Flagging</h2>

  <p>
    Every report, debate and comment can be flagged from the page it appears on. Nothing is
    read by a moderator before it is published, so flagging is how anything gets looked at:
    a flag is not a complaint into a void, it is what starts a decision. If it concerns a
    moderator, or you would rather it did not go through the site, write to
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tell us what you saw and where. You do not have to explain why it upset you, and you
    will not be asked to justify flagging something.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens next</h2>

  <p>
    Moderation here is volunteer-run. That means it is careful rather than fast, and it is
    honest to say so up front: a flag may take days. Content that looks like it could do
    real harm is hidden while it is looked at, and hiding something is not a finding
    against anyone.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Every decision comes with an explanation, and both sides read it.</strong> When a
    moderator answers a flag they have to write down why, and that sentence is shown to the
    person who wrote the content and to the person who flagged it, under
    <em>moderation decisions</em> on their account pages. It never says who flagged
    something, and it never names the moderator: the decision is the site's, not one
    person's.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every decision is also recorded — who took it, when, and why — in a log the moderators
    keep on each other. Hiding something preserves it: the text and the name stay, its author
    can see that it was hidden, and it can be put back. A hidden report can be edited by its
    author, which is how an explanation gets answered rather than merely received.
  </p>

  <p>Depending on what happened, the response is one of:</p>

  <ul class="tombstone-list">
    <li>The content stays up, with the reason written to both of you.</li>
    <li>The content hidden, with the thread structure left intact and the reason written to both of you.</li>
    <li>
      The account suspended, with the reason written to whoever holds it. A suspension stops
      you posting and leaves everything you have already posted where it is. It can be lifted.
    </li>
    <li>The account removed.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If your content is hidden or your account is suspended, you are told what rule was
    applied and why, in writing, on your account page. You can reply, and a decision will be
    reconsidered if you have reason to think it was wrong. Deanonymisation and harassment do
    not get a warning first.
  </p>

  <h2>If your account is suspended</h2>

  <p>
    Most decisions here are about one thing somebody posted. A suspension is different: it is
    about an account rather than about a post, and it is used for the two patterns that hiding
    cannot answer — <strong>spam</strong>, meaning the same promotion posted over and over, and
    <strong>sustained hostility</strong> toward other people. One badly judged report is not a
    suspension. Being wrong is not a suspension, and neither is being negative about a tool
    everyone else likes.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>A suspension is not a lockout.</strong> You keep your account and you keep your
    access to it. You can still sign in, read everything on the site, change your display name
    and bio, change your password, and ask for the account to be erased. What stops is writing:
    while a suspension is in place you cannot post a report, suggest a debate, submit anything to
    the network, comment, rate a debate, confirm whether a report still reproduces, flag anything, or
    reference one page from another.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Nothing you have already posted is removed.</strong> Your reports stay in the corpus,
    your comments stay in their threads, and your name and badge stay on them. Taking a post down
    is a separate decision about that post, made for its own reasons and explained on its own. A
    suspension is about what happens next, not a verdict on your past work.
  </p>

  <p>You find out in three places, all of them on this site:</p>

  <ul class="tombstone-list">
    <li>A notice at the top of your account page, saying the account is suspended.</li>
    <li>
      The moderator's written explanation under <em>moderation decisions</em>, which is the same
      sentence the moderators keep for themselves. It does not name the moderator, and it does
      not name whoever flagged anything.
    </li>
    <li>A line in your activity, linking to that explanation.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>We do not email you about it</strong>, and you should know that rather than discover
    it. Nothing about a suspension reaches you unless you come back to the site and look. That is
    a real gap and it is on the list to fix; in the meantime, if your account has stopped letting
    you post and you were not expecting it, the explanation is waiting on your account page.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>You can appeal, and you should if you think it is wrong.</strong> Write to
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>. Wherever there is a second
    moderator to ask, the reply comes from someone other than the person who decided. A
    suspension can be lifted, and lifting it is its own decision with its own explanation — you
    are told when it ends and why, not left to test it by trying to post.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Nobody else can see it.</strong> There is no mark on your profile, no badge on your
    contributions, and no public list of suspended accounts. A reader looking at your author page
    sees an ordinary contributor, because that is what your work is. This is deliberate: many
    people here contribute under a pseudonym precisely because admitting to heavy AI use carries a
    professional cost, and a permanent public mark is not a sanction volunteers answering flags
    should be able to impose.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two last things about who can do it. A moderator cannot suspend their own account, and cannot
    suspend another moderator — that takes direct access to the database, so that one stolen
    session cannot silence the people who would notice. And a suspension always carries an
    explanation: a decision nobody has to write down is not one this site can take.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>

  <p>
    This document will change as the site meets situations nobody anticipated. Changes are
    dated at the top and their history is in the
    <a href={SITE.repository}>public repository</a>. The
    <a href={path('/privacy/')}>privacy notice</a> covers what is stored about you, including
    what happens to your content if you leave.
  </p>
</Page>

